.TH tusplit n 0.3 tclutils
.SH tclutils::tusplit
.PP
Split files or strings into smaller parts.
.SS Load
.CS
package require tclutils::tusplit 0.3
.CE
.SS "Line splitting"
.CS
set files [::tclutils::tusplit::file input.txt -lines 1000 -outdir parts -prefix part -suffix .txt]
.CE
.SS "Byte splitting"
.CS
set files [::tclutils::tusplit::file input.bin -bytes 1048576 -outdir parts -prefix chunk]
.CE
.SS Commands
.IP \(bu
\fBsplitLines text ?options?\fR
.IP \(bu
\fBsplitBytes data ?options?\fR
.IP \(bu
\fBfile path ?options?\fR
.IP \(bu
\fBlines path n ?options?\fR
.IP \(bu
\fBbytes path n ?options?\fR
.SS Options
.CS
-lines n     lines per part (splitLines, file; default 1000)
-bytes n     bytes per part (splitBytes; default 1024); with file it
             switches to byte mode
-prefix p    start of the file name (default x)
-suffix s    end of the file name (default empty)
-digits n    digits of the running number, from 0 (default 2)
-outdir d    target directory, created if missing (default .)
-encoding e  splitLines: encoding of the parts (default utf-8)
.CE
.PP
Without \fB\-lines\fR and \fB\-bytes\fR, \fBfile\fR splits by 1000 lines. Parts are named \fBx00\fR, \fBx01\fR, ... Every line keeps its line end, so the parts put together give the input again, as with \fBsplit \-l\fR (0.3; up to 0.2 each part ended without \fB\en\fR). \fBfile \-lines\fR works on the bytes of the file: CRLF and the encoding stay as they are. \fBsplitLines\fR writes the text in \fB\-encoding\fR; an unknown encoding raises \fBTCLUTILS TUSPLIT ENCODING name\fR.
.PP
\fBsplitBytes\fR expects a byte string: every character must be in \fB\ex00\fR..\fB\exFF\fR. Convert text first, e.g. \fBencoding convertto utf\-8 $text\fR. A character above \fB\exFF\fR raises \fBTCLUTILS TUSPLIT BYTES\fR before any file is written (same under Tcl 8.6 and 9).
.SS Errors
.CS
-lines and -bytes together (file)   TCLUTILS TUSPLIT MODE
-outdir is a file                   TCLUTILS TUSPLIT OUTDIR
character above \xFF (splitBytes)   TCLUTILS TUSPLIT BYTES
count or -digits below 1            TCLUTILS COMMON INTEGER -name
unknown option / missing value      TCLUTILS COMMON OPTION -name
.CE
